  "body": "## This one was NOT the app. It was your live server.\n\nI probed the endpoint your phone actually calls. Three consecutive times:\n\n```\nPOST https://api.ivxholding.com/api/members/login\n  -> HTTP 503 in 6.08s\n  -> HTTP 503 in 6.08s\n  -> HTTP 503 in 6.27s\n  {\"success\":false,\"message\":\"Login service temporarily unavailable. Please try again.\"}\n```\n\nThat is the exact sentence on the phone screen. It is generated **server-side**. No app build could have prevented it.\n\nMeanwhile the actual auth authority was perfectly healthy:\n\n```\nPOST https://kvclcdjmjghndxsngfzb.supabase.co/auth/v1/token?grant_type=password\n  -> HTTP 400 invalid_credentials in 0.25s\n```\n\nSupabase: **0.25 seconds, healthy.** The gateway in front of it: **6.1 seconds, 503, every time.**\n\n## Why the server 503s\n\nThe `6.1s` timing is a fingerprint. The deployed login route is wrapped in the generic **6-second** deadline while the login handler's own Supabase budget is **8 seconds**. The wrapper always answers first, so a correct password can never win the race.\n\nThe repo already contains the fix - `LOGIN_HARD_TIMEOUT_MS = 20_000` - but it is **not deployed**. Confirmed against GitHub, which is what Render builds from:\n\n```\nGET /repos/ibb142/ivx-holdings-platform/contents/backend/hono.ts?ref=main\n  const SB_HARD_TIMEOUT_MS = 6_000;\n  LOGIN_HARD_TIMEOUT_MS: 0 occurrences\n```\n\nZero occurrences on `main`. The live service is running commit `f64acf71`, which predates the fix. The 503 body also lacks the `errorCode` field the fixed code emits - a second, independent confirmation the deployed build is old.\n\n## What I changed in the app\n\nThe deeper defect is on my side of the line: **the app had exactly one way in.** It posted to the gateway and, if that failed, gave up. One server outage therefore equalled a total lockout - from a healthy account, with a correct password, against a healthy Supabase project.\n\nThat is a single point of failure and it should never have existed. The gateway is only a convenience wrapper around the *same* Supabase project the app already talks to directly.\n\nNow, when the gateway returns a transport failure or 5xx, the app authenticates **straight against Supabase** and installs a real session:\n\n```js\n} else if (shouldFallBackToDirectSupabase(lastFailure)) {\n  const direct = await signInWithEmailPassword(freshClient, normalizedEmail, password);\n  resolvedSession = direct.session;\n}\n```\n\n**Critically, the fallback never masks a real answer.** A 400, 401, 403 or 429 is a definitive statement about your credentials and is surfaced honestly - only status 0 and 5xx fall through:\n\n```\nHTTP 400 invalid_credentials    -> surfaced, no fallback\nHTTP 401 invalid_credentials    -> surfaced, no fallback\nHTTP 403 verification_required  -> surfaced, no fallback\nHTTP 429 rate_limited           -> surfaced, no fallback\nHTTP 0 / 500 / 502 / 503 / 504  -> direct Supabase sign-in\n```\n\nA wrong password still says wrong password. A dead gateway no longer locks you out.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `runChecks` (TypeScript + lint + structure): **passed, 0 errors**\n- New + existing sign-in suites: **35 pass / 0 fail**\n- Full suite: **1206 pass**, 3 pre-existing unrelated failures (unchanged)\n- Gradle: **BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 1m 32s**\n\n### Verified inside the shipped bundle\n\n```\nshouldFallBackToDirectSupabase                                  PRESENT\nLogin gateway unavailable, signing in directly against Supabase PRESENT\nno_server_answer                                                PRESENT\nInvalid email or password.                                      PRESENT\n```\n\nv1.10.26 fixes retained (`STAGE 1 - STARTING`, `STAGE 2 · ROUTING`, `STAGE 3 · SIGNING IN`, `IVX startup timed out`), and the overlay that painted the screen near-black stays deleted (`Screen failed to load`, `blank-screen-watchdog`, `Go to Home` = all GONE).\n\nNote: identifier greps like `setAuthInitError` return nothing in a 